title: "THE COTTON LINE: Testing the Persistence of an 1853 Colonial Land-Tenure Boundary in Present-Day Vidarbha"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/THE-COTTON-LINE"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/THE-COTTON-LINE"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.22111298"
    description: "Zenodo archive of THE COTTON LINE v1.0.1"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22111298"
abstract: >-
  A historical-institutional and spatial-discontinuity study testing whether an 1853 colonial
  land-tenure boundary in Vidarbha, Maharashtra -- Ryotwari cotton country (Berar) to the west,
  Malguzari mixed-tenure country (Central Provinces) to the east, administratively abolished in
  1956 -- still registers as a measurable present-day signature in cotton-crop phenology, agrarian
  labor structure, land use, and land-surface temperature. The modern boundary was built by
  dissolving 120 talukas across Vidarbha's 11 districts into their two historical-division
  successors and extracting the shared 384.7 km edge, then verified against documented district
  administrative lineage from 1903-1956 rather than a period map, since no accessible digitized
  one exists. Four outcomes were tested with an OLS spatial-discontinuity model (outcome ~ side +
  signed_dist_km + side:signed_dist_km + annual_total_mm + elevation_m) at both a 32-taluka
  immediate-boundary corridor (+/-25 km) and a 66-68-taluka full-scene footprint. No outcome
  showed a statistically significant level jump right at the historical line at either scale once
  rainfall and elevation were controlled for. A real regional gradient in kharif-to-rabi NDVI
  decline was found instead -- a 0.070-point Berar-side excess at the full-footprint scale,
  independently reproduced at a 0.075-point gap a year later on a second Landsat cycle -- with the
  same side x distance interaction independently significant for land-surface temperature
  (p=0.0105) alongside NDVI (p=0.0348). Agrarian labor structure showed a scale-sensitive result: a
  significant slope-difference interaction at the corridor scale (p=0.0361, stable in direction
  across a 32-run leave-one-out jackknife) that flips to a significant level jump at the
  full-footprint scale (p=0.014), traced to the satellite scene's asymmetric district coverage
  rather than treated as a contradiction. A field ground-truth check at seven boundaries in Karanja
  Ghadge and Sawardoh villages cross-validated the satellite NDVI signal against Sentinel-2 and
  dated ground photography. The evidence supports a gradient-based, rather than discontinuous,
  form of institutional persistence in this landscape.
keywords:
  - institutional persistence
  - spatial discontinuity
  - historical GIS
  - land tenure
  - NDVI
  - land surface temperature
  - agrarian labor structure
  - remote sensing
  - Vidarbha
  - regression discontinuity
  - colonial land revenue systems
  - India
